%N A071513 Sprague-Grundy values for octal game .3737.
%C A071513 Octal games .6jTk, j=0,1,2,3, T=6,7, k=0,1,2,3 have values a(n-1). Contrary to Winning Ways, .37371 does not have the same values as this sequence. - _Eric M. Schmidt_, Mar 01 2017
%C A071513 From _Tyler Satchel Orden_, Jul 30 2026: (Start)
%C A071513 a(n) is also the Sprague-Grundy value of a heap of n+1 tokens in the octal game .606; equivalently, the Sprague-Grundy sequence of .606 is 0, 0, followed by this sequence.
%C A071513 Computed (as the game .606) through heap size 2^29 = 536870912 by Tyler Satchel Orden (2026), extending A. Flammenkamp's tables for .606, which reached 2^28. All of Flammenkamp's published invariants at 2^28 (53811 rare values in his sparse-space sense, last at heap size 268412360; maximum value 1598, first at heap size 245416760) were reproduced exactly before extending. Beyond 2^28 the sparse set remains active: 8162 further rare values appear, the last at heap size 536867468, all with values in the interval [1026, 1069]; the maximum value grows to 1610, first attained at heap size 471189721. (End)
%D A071513 E. R. Berlekamp, J. H. Conway and R. K. Guy, Winning Ways, Academic Press, NY, 2 vols., 1982; see Chapter 4.
%H A071513 Eric M. Schmidt, <a href="/A071513/b071513.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
